// Runtime/ProcessRuntime.maxon — the process family's COMPILER-SIDE VOCABULARY: the three entry-point names
// the parser emits calls to, the band test that decides which targets may run them, and the ONE body still
// built as IR here.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT THE FAMILY SERVES — THREE `__Builtins` INTRINSICS ABOUT THE RUNNING PROCESS.**
// `stdlib/Process.maxon`'s `executablePath()` bottoms out in `__Builtins.executablePath()`;
// `Compiler/TreeLock.maxon`'s lock token bottoms out in `__Builtins.currentProcessId()`; and
// `Main.runSpecTest` calls `__Builtins.enterBackgroundPriority()` before it spawns a worker pool. Only the
// first has a stdlib wrapper: a declaration whose whole body is one intrinsic call is the thin wrapper the
// house rules forbid. Nothing else in the language reaches these entry points.
//
// ⛔⛔ **TWO OF THE THREE BODIES ARE RUNTIME SOURCE AND THIS ONE IS NOT, AND THE LINE IS A MECHANISM RATHER
// THAN AN APPETITE.** `runtime/Process.maxon` writes `__proc_pid` and `__proc_bg_priority`, each one `__Raw`
// op the tier can spell. `__proc_exe_path` cannot follow yet: it allocates a raw buffer, grows it and builds
// an `__ManagedMemory` answer, so as tier source it would CALL other runtime entries — and `scanRuntimeUsage`
// walks every runtime body in every program, so a call inside one would set that family's bit for a program
// dead-function elimination sweeps the body out of. That vacuity holds only while every tier body calls
// nothing but `__Raw`, which names no callee. The allocator's own migration is what answers it.
//
// ⭐ **TWO HOST FACILITIES, AND WHICH TARGETS HAVE EACH IS `TargetFacilities`' TO SAY.** A target that has
// neither refuses at the call site with the EXISTING `E3104 targetUnsupportedConstruct`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE `__proc_` PREFIX DOES NOT ANSWER FOR THE WHOLE BAND.** The two IDENTITY reads answer
// `processInfo` by that prefix; the PRIORITY write answers `processPriority`, which `calleeHostFacility`
// tests for by NAME ahead of the band. The split records a difference in KIND rather than a schedule:
// reading an identity is one call that cannot fail, while the write's POSIX scope disagrees —
// `setpriority` scopes to the process on Darwin and to the calling THREAD on Linux — so a lane can serve
// the reads without serving the write, and one answer for three entries would make the whole band wait on
// the narrowest of them. ⇒ **A fourth entry point is still covered by construction, and it will answer
// `processInfo`** — which is right for another identity read and wrong for another write, so a write joins
// by name.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE REASON THE PATH WALK IS A LOOP HERE RATHER THAN A LOWERING: THERE ARE THREE DIFFERENT OS APIS
// AND THEY DO NOT AGREE ON THEIR OWN SHAPE.** `stdlib/Process.maxon:26-27` names all three —
// `GetModuleFileNameA` on Windows, `_NSGetExecutablePath` on macOS, `/proc/self/exe` on Linux — and they
// differ in more than spelling: the first FILLS a caller's buffer and answers a count, the second takes an
// IN-OUT size, signals "too small" through a `-1` return plus a rewritten size, leaves that size UNCHANGED
// on success (so the length must be MEASURED) and answers a path that "may be a symbolic link and not the
// real file", the third is a SYMLINK that must be `readlink`'d and is not NUL-terminated. Those are three
// retry protocols behind one surface, and the compiler's Std tier is TARGET-NEUTRAL: there is no place in it
// for a body that is a doubling loop on one target and a readlink on another. So each backend translates its
// own convention into `osExecutablePath`'s, and the loop below is written once — which is what the arm64
// lane actually did (`Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinExePathChunk`), with no edit to this file.
// ⚠ wasm is refused for a STRONGER reason than any of those: WASI has no executable-path primitive AT ALL.
// A component has no image path to report, and `args_get[0]` is whatever the host chose to pass rather than
// a resolved path — so a lowering there could only be a plausible WRONG ANSWER, which is worse than a
// refusal because `File.exists` on it would simply say false and the program would blame itself.

// The `__proc_` prefix that identifies every process runtime entry point, and the one `SemanticCheck` reads
// for E3104 through `TargetFacilities.calleeHostFacility`.
let ProcessRuntimePrefix = b"__proc_"

// ⭐ **THE THREE ENTRY POINTS.** Only the first is built below; the other two are declared in
// `runtime/Process.maxon` and reached by a call the parser emits from the `__Builtins` spelling.
export let ProcessExecutablePathName = b"__proc_exe_path"
export let ProcessCurrentPidName = b"__proc_pid"

// ⭐ **THE ONE THE PREFIX DOES NOT ANSWER FOR.** It is refused by E3104 on a target that cannot lower
// it, but through `processPriority` rather than `processInfo` — see the header for why a WRITE whose scope
// differs per platform cannot be fused to the two identity reads.
export let ProcessBackgroundPriorityName = b"__proc_bg_priority"

// The first buffer the walk asks for is `ManagedDirectoryRuntime.MaxPathBytes` — the same `MAX_PATH` the
// `GetCurrentDirectoryA` walk starts at, READ from there rather than re-spelled here (W5 review), because
// what 260 means is a Win32 fact and not this loop's policy. `MAX_PATH` fits every path a program is
// normally installed at, so the overwhelmingly common case is one call and one allocation — and it is not a
// LIMIT here the way it is in an API that refuses past it, because the loop below grows past it. A
// larger start would cost twice the allocation on every ordinary path to save one doubling on a
// long one.

// ⚠ **THE GROWTH FACTOR MOVED TO `ManagedDirectoryRuntime.PathGrowthShift` (MAC5)**, beside the
// `MaxPathBytes` this loop already reads from there: `__md_current_path` grows the same way now that
// `getcwd` has replaced a `GetCurrentDirectoryA` that could name its own required size, and the first
// attempt's size and the growth step are ONE policy rather than two facts in two files.
//
// ⚠ **THE OTHER LANE COULD HAVE ANSWERED A SIZE AND DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.** `_NSGetExecutablePath` rewrites
// its in-out size to the one it needs, so an arm64-only loop could retry exactly once. `osExecutablePath`
// carries no channel for that number — deliberately, because a channel only one lane can fill would make
// this target-neutral graph ask which target it is compiling for — so that lane answers `bufLen`, i.e.
// "too small", and pays at most one extra doubling. See `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinExePathChunk`.

// ⭐⭐ **`__proc_exe_path() -> arrRec`** — a FRESH owned `__ManagedMemory` holding the running image's
// absolute path, and the caller frees it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS A REAL LOOP, WHERE `__md_current_path`'s RETRY IS STRAIGHT-LINE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE
// API's NOT THE AUTHOR's.** `GetCurrentDirectoryA` answers the size it NEEDS when the buffer is too small,
// so one retry at exactly that size is provably enough and a second failure would be the OS contradicting
// itself. `GetModuleFileNameA` does not: on truncation it answers `nSize` — the size it was GIVEN — so
// there is no size to retry at, only a bigger guess. Doubling is therefore the only available policy and
// the number of doublings is not bounded by anything this code knows, which makes a loop the honest
// structure rather than a stylistic choice.
//
// ⭐ **THE THREE ANSWERS ARE CLASSIFIED BY THE SHARED VERDICT, NOT BY A SECOND COPY OF IT** (W5 review).
// `0` is a failure; `< capacity` means the path is in the buffer, NUL-terminated; anything else means
// truncation — and `written == 0` must be asked FIRST, because a 0 also satisfies `< capacity`. That is one
// fact about a Win32 buffer fill and it now lives in one place,
// `ManagedDirectoryRuntime.emitFilledBufferVerdict`; only where each of the three answers GOES is this
// loop's own. Here the third grows and goes round again, where `__md_current_path`'s retries once at the
// size the OS named.
//
// ⚠ **A FAILURE ANSWERS THE EMPTY BUFFER RATHER THAN THROWING**, which is the intrinsic's documented
// contract (`/specs/builtins-type.md`: *"empty buffer when unavailable"*) and matches `__cl_arg`, whose
// out-of-range index is answered rather than refused. It is spelled by NUL-terminating the buffer at
// offset 0 and then joining the ORDINARY copy-out — one producer of the answer, whatever the reason for it,
// which is exactly how `__cl_arg` spells its own out-of-range case.
// ⚠ It is also unreachable in practice on either lane: `GetModuleFileNameA(NULL, …)` and
// `_NSGetExecutablePath` both ask about the image that is currently executing, and the only documented
// failure of either is a buffer too small — which the loop answers rather than surfaces. The arm is here
// because "the OS cannot fail" is not something a runtime gets to assume, not because a test drives it.
//
// ⚠ The raw buffer is a scratch allocation this function owns end to end: it is freed on the one exit, and
// what the caller receives is the independent `__ManagedMemory` copy.
function buildProcessExecutablePath(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ProcessExecutablePathName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var attempt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxattempt", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fitTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxfit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxgrow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unavailable = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxnone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var copyOut = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxcopy", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// The buffer and its capacity are the loop's carried pair — the phi model is the only way a value
	// crosses a merge at this tier, and `attempt` is the only block with more than one predecessor.
	let buffer = ids.mint()
	let capacity = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(attempt, values: twoArgs(buffer, second: capacity))

	let firstSize = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: MaxPathBytes, ids: ids)
	let firstBuf = emitRawAlloc(module, block: entry, byteCount: firstSize, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(attempt.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(attempt.id, argIds: twoArgs(firstBuf, second: firstSize)))

	// ⚠ `fitTest`, `grow`, `unavailable` and `copyOut` carry NO block args and take no edge arguments:
	// `attempt` dominates all four (it is the only way into any of them), so `buffer`, `capacity` and
	// `written` are simply in scope — the dominance argument `emitCStringLength` states for its own length.
	let written = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(attempt, op: StdOp.osExecutablePath(written, bufLenId: capacity, bufPtrId: buffer))

	// The three-way classification is `ManagedDirectoryRuntime.emitFilledBufferVerdict`'s, not a second copy
	// of it: `GetModuleFileNameA` and `GetCurrentDirectoryA` disagree about what "too small" MEANS (nSize
	// against the size needed) and about what to do next, and agree exactly about how to TELL the three
	// answers apart — including that `written == 0` must be asked before the fit test, because a 0 also
	// satisfies `< capacity`. No edges are pushed here: `attempt` dominates all four exits, so the pair it
	// carries is simply in scope and nothing crosses these merges.
	emitFilledBufferVerdict(module, block: attempt, check: fitTest, written: written, capacity: capacity, whenRefused: unavailable, whenFits: copyOut, whenTooSmall: grow, ids: ids)

	// Truncated: release this attempt's buffer, double the capacity and go round again.
	emitRawFree(module, block: grow, ptr: buffer, ids: ids)
	let shift = emitConst(module, block: grow, value: PathGrowthShift, ids: ids)
	let grownCapacity = emitBin(module, block: grow, a: capacity, b: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let grownBuf = emitRawAlloc(module, block: grow, byteCount: grownCapacity, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(grow, op: StdOp.branch(attempt.id))
	grow.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(attempt.id, argIds: twoArgs(grownBuf, second: grownCapacity)))

	// Unavailable: make the buffer the empty string and take the ordinary copy-out.
	let emptyAt = emitConst(module, block: unavailable, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyByte = emitConst(module, block: unavailable, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: unavailable, base: buffer, index: emptyAt, value: emptyByte, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unavailable, op: StdOp.branch(copyOut.id))

	let record = ids.mint()
	var built = emitCStringToManaged(module, func: func, cur: copyOut, cstr: buffer, record: record, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: built, ptr: buffer, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(built, op: StdOp.ret(record))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildProcessExecutablePath'

// ⚠ **THE GUARD IS PER ENTRY, NOT PER FILE**, and one entry is left to guard: the path walk drags in
// `usesHeap` AND `usesManagedMemory`, i.e. the whole managed-memory runtime installed as one closure, so a
// program whose only introspection is a pid must not reach it. The other two entries need no guard here at
// all — their bodies are tier source, and dead-function elimination decides whether each survives.
export function installProcessRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if usage.usesExecutablePath 'executablePath'
		buildProcessExecutablePath(module)
	end 'executablePath'
end 'installProcessRuntime'

// ⭐ **THE FAMILY'S BAND, AND IT ANSWERS FOR THE `processInfo` HALF.** `calleeHostFacility` reads it AFTER
// its by-name test for the priority write, so the write never reaches here. A PREFIX test rather than an
// `equals` against a roster of names, for `isCommandLineRuntimeCallee`'s reason: a further identity read is
// then covered by construction instead of having to be remembered at two sites.
// `specs/process-id.md:rejected-on-wasm` is the case that pins the E3104 `__proc_pid` earns by prefix alone.
//
// ⚠ **`scanRuntimeUsage` DOES NOT READ THIS ONE.** It asks for the path walk by NAME, because a prefix is
// the right test for "may this target run it at all" — one answer for the family — and the wrong test for
// "must this program carry it", which is per entry point and costs wildly different amounts here.
export function isProcessRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ProcessRuntimePrefix)
end 'isProcessRuntimeCallee'
